𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟱𝟱/𝟯𝟲𝟱 — 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝗢𝗽𝘀 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗖𝗵𝗮𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗻𝗴𝗲

🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗣𝗼𝗱𝘀 𝗮𝗿𝗲 𝗣𝗲𝗻𝗱𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗗𝘂𝗲 𝘁𝗼 𝗜𝗻𝘀𝘂𝗳𝗳𝗶𝗰𝗶𝗲𝗻𝘁 𝗥𝗲𝘀𝗼𝘂𝗿𝗰𝗲𝘀 — 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗪𝗼𝘂𝗹𝗱 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗙𝗶𝘅 𝗜𝘁?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

Your application is receiving more traffic. HPA increases the replica count, but the newly created Pods remain in "Pending" state. How would you troubleshoot this in production?

Here’s my practical approach 👇

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟭: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗦𝘁𝗮𝘁𝘂𝘀

kubectl get pods -n <namespace> -o wide
kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Check the Events section for messages such as:

- "Insufficient cpu"
- "Insufficient memory"
- "Too many pods"
- Node selector or affinity mismatch

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟮: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗡𝗼𝗱𝗲 𝗖𝗮𝗽𝗮𝗰𝗶𝘁𝘆

kubectl get nodes
kubectl describe nodes
kubectl top nodes

Review node allocatable CPU and memory, existing workloads, and available capacity.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟯: 𝗥𝗲𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗥𝗲𝘀𝗼𝘂𝗿𝗰𝗲 𝗥𝗲𝗾𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝘀

kubectl get deployment <deployment-name> -n <namespace> -o yaml

Check CPU and memory requests.

Remember: Kubernetes scheduling is primarily based on resource requests, not simply the current resource usage shown by monitoring tools.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟰: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗡𝗼𝗱𝗲 𝗦𝗰𝗵𝗲𝗱𝘂𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝘀𝘁𝗿𝗮𝗶𝗻𝘁𝘀

Review:

- Node selectors
- Node affinity and anti-affinity
- Taints and tolerations
- Topology spread constraints

A node may have free resources but still be unsuitable for the Pod.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟱: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗖𝗹𝘂𝘀𝘁𝗲𝗿 𝗔𝘂𝘁𝗼𝘀𝗰𝗮𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗴

If the cluster uses Cluster Autoscaler, verify whether it can add worker nodes and whether node-group limits or cloud quotas are blocking scale-up.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟲: 𝗩𝗲𝗿𝗶𝗳𝘆 𝗦𝗰𝗵𝗲𝗱𝘂𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗴

kubectl get pods -n <namespace> -o wide
kubectl get events -n <namespace> --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp

Confirm that the Pods are scheduled successfully and become Ready.

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

HPA scales Pods, but it does not guarantee that the cluster has enough capacity to run them.

Always investigate the complete path:

HPA → Pod creation → Scheduler → Worker Node → Application readiness.
